course: dictionary
lesson: 2427
title: Czech: a country, a person, a language
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Czech can work in three ways: as a describing word, as a name for a person, and as a name for a language. The clues in the sentence tell you which one is meant. «The Czech Republic» is a country in central Europe, so Czech here means from or of that country.
explain: «Ukrainian, Polish and Czech are cousins in the Slavic family.» Here Czech names a language, just as Polish and Ukrainian do. A Czech is a person from the Czech Republic, which is also called Czechia.
explain: Czech is a proper noun, so it always starts with a capital C, even when it describes things like food or language. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. If «from the Czech Republic» still fits, you have understood it.
fact: Czech means from or of the Czech Republic (Czechia), a country in central Europe.
fact: A Czech is a person from the Czech Republic.
fact: Czech is also the language spoken in the Czech Republic.
fact: The capital of the Czech Republic is Prague.
fact: Czech always has a capital C, because it is a proper noun.
exercise: «Lego has factories in Denmark, Hungary, the Czech Republic, Mexico, China and Vietnam.» In this sentence, what does the Czech Republic name? | *A country in central Europe | A type of Lego brick | A kind of old lift | A language spoken by children
exercise: «Ukrainian, Polish and Czech are cousins in the Slavic family.» In this sentence, what does Czech name? | A type of old lift | A famous brick | *A language | A capital city
exercise: Lucie wrote, «I love czech food.» What is wrong? | It needs a question mark at the end | *It needs a capital C, because Czech is a proper noun | Nothing is wrong | It should say Czech food with a full stop before food
test: «A paternoster is an old kind of lift... a few still work in Germany and the Czech Republic.» What is the capital of the Czech Republic? | Warsaw | Vienna | Berlin | *Prague
test: Mara is a ___ from Prague. Which word fits the gap? | Dutch | Spanish | Latin | *Czech
exam: Which sentence uses Czech correctly? | Our teacher is czech. | *Last year I visited the Czech Republic. | I visited the czech Republic last year. | Czech is a famous czech bread.
